  "worked_solution": "Recall that the $xy$-coordinate plane is called the *phase plane* of the system. A particular solution of an initial value problem is called a *trajectory* (or *orbit*).\n\nFirst, we compute the point that corresponds to $t=\\operatorname{ln}\u20612$ on the given trajectory:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612) & ={e}^{2(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612)}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{3(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612)}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+8[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the equation of our system, we obtain the tangent vector to the trajectory.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(\\operatorname{ln}\u20612) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2(12)+1(8) \\\\ 0(12)+3(8)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}24+8 \\\\ 0+24\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}32 \\\\ 24\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
